  "strategy": "**Approach and rationale**\n\nWe will use a combination of multi-start coordinate descent and iterative refinement to minimize the objective function. The central idea is to generate multiple initial sequences representing different physical waveforms (sharp peaks, flat, Gaussian-like) and optimize them using a coordinate descent strategy adapted to the non-convex nature of the problem. Unlike the parent step functions, we will focus on sequences that have localized peaks and valleys to smooth out the objective function landscape. The optimization uses a grid-based searching for each coordinate, evaluating the impact of incremental changes to minimize the objective while ensuring non-negative coefficients. \n\nWe address the parent's constraint directly by treating the given parent sequence as a physical structure that can be optimized further. We also introduce a novel coordinate update method that considers the smoothing properties of these step functions by incorporating FFT-based convolution checks. This approach explicitly pursues the minimization of the peak convolution values by focusing updates on coordinates that contribute most to the peaks, while carefully avoiding overstepping the time budget through structured parallel evaluations.\n\n**Mathematical specification**\n\nWe work with `a` as a normalized sequence (sum = 1) of `n` non-negative coefficients, bounded by 0 and 1. The objective function is:\n\n```\nO(a) = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a)) / sum(a)^2\n```\n\nBut note: by using a normalized sequence (sum=1), `O(a) = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a))`.\n\nThe full auto-correlation is computed using `np.convolve(a, a)`, yielding a result of length `2 * n - 1`. The maximum of these values, together with their index positions, drives the optimization. Each update to coordinate `i` must ensure we do not increase the maximum convolution value disproportionately.\n\n**Implementation procedure**\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - Load the parent sequence `height_sequence_1` if available.\n   - Convert the initial sequence into a normalized vector for optimization (project_simplex).\n   - Compute the initial objective value by evaluating the sequence.\n\n2. Grid Convolution Approach:\n   - For the given sequence length `n` (>0), generate four types of initial sequences:\n        a. Average-normal (baseline)\n        b. Sharp central peaks (central mass)\n        c. Gaussian-like profile (smooth decay)\n        d. Alternating peaks-and-valleys (oscillating structure)\n   - For each initial sequence, compute its objective value.\n\n3. Coordinate Descent Optimization:\n   - For each initial sequence, perform coordinate descent iterations:\n        a. For each coordinate `i` in the sequence, consider a grid of allowed values (from 0 to current value + 50% maximum change).\n        b. Only allow updates that maintain non-negative and normalized (sum=1) properties.\n        c. After updating coordinate `i`, compute the full convolution (using fast_convolve) and the new maximum value.\n        d. Check if the new maximum is an improvement (or degradation) based on the original value and the update's effect.\n\n4. Parallel Evaluations:\n   - Use multiprocessing to evaluate each initial sequence type concurrently.\n   - In each iteration, update all coordinates simultaneously and re-normalize.\n\n5. Objective Tracking:\n   - Maintain a global variable `best_sequence` and `best_objective` from all candidates.\n   - Evaluate each candidate immediately after generation.\n\n6. Local Search Enhancement:\n   - After the main optimization loop terminates, perform a local search by swapping mass between coordinates, using the original gradient method to identify peak contributions.\n\n7. Time Budget Handling:\n   - Allocate 50% of the time budget for initial parallel searches (for `n` and for parent sequence).\n   - Use 80% of the remaining budget for coordinate descent updates.\n\n**Search choices and useful variations**\n\n- We will evaluate at least four initial sequences for each unique length found.\n  - These lengths include `n` from parent, 1024, 1280, 1536.\n\n- During coordinate descent, we will:\n   a. Search from 0 to 100% of the initial coordinate value (or 0) with up to 5 grid points.\n   b. Mostly experiment with fluctuation-based restarts and momentum if necessary.\n\n- We'll use a parallel evaluation for each unique sequence length found, hoping to cover diverse waveform solutions.\n\n**Computational budget and robustness**\n\n- Number of initial candidates: 4.\n- Each coordinate descent iteration: 5 steps (from 0 to update).\n- Total iterations budget: 1800 seconds.\n- Summary:\n    - Phase 1: Initial candidate generation (`n<=4` sequences).\n    - Phase 2: Coordinate descent optimization with parallel evaluation.\n    - Phase 3: Local search until the global objective stops improving.\n\n**Validation and return contract**\n\n- Validate every generated sequence by re-normalizing (project_simplex).\n- The returned sequence should be the candidate with the best objective value found during the optimization.\n- Print progress every 50 iterations and log the best improvement.\n\nThe algorithm is complete and will return the best sequence found after careful optimization.",
